From: sheng-development
Subject: [PATCH] Verify a fingerprint alongside the password prompt

PAM is a single conversation, and pam_fprintd holds it for its whole timeout,
so a greeter with the reader in its stack cannot look at a password until the
scan gives up:

	15:42:14.090  "Place your finger on the fingerprint reader"
	15:42:44.083  "Verification timed out"
	15:42:44.228  Authentication for user "sushy" successful

The 145 ms at the end is the password, typed thirty seconds earlier and held
until the stack got to it. KWin's lock screen avoids this by running a second,
fingerprint-only PAM service (kde-fingerprint) beside the password one and
taking whichever answers first. SDDM has no equivalent, and upstream ships
kde-fingerprint for unlocking only, never for login.

Give SDDM the same shape. A second Auth drives an sddm-fingerprint service in
its own helper, started once the greeter is up and re-armed when fprintd times
out, so the reader stays live for as long as the greeter is on screen. The two
conversations are independent: whichever succeeds starts the session and stops
the other.

	[Fingerprint]
	Enable=true
	User=       # empty: whoever logged in last, which the greeter preselects

Based on sddm/sddm#1220, which has sat unmerged since 2019, but reworked
rather than carried. That version shares one Auth between both conversations,
so the second silently cancels the first and orphans a helper still holding
the fprintd claim; it needs the account named in sddm.conf up front instead of
following the last-logged-in user; it overloads submitting an empty password
as "try the finger"; and it narrows Auth's finished() to fire only on success,
which changes failure handling for every login, not just this one.

Deliberately not carried: the PAM file it adds, which is Arch-shaped
(system-local-login, pam_faillock) and whose install rule does not apply to
0.21.0 -- that release guards those installs with INSTALL_PAM_CONFIGURATION.
NixOS builds its stacks from security.pam.services, so the service is defined
in modules/greeter.nix instead.

A fingerprint login leaves KWallet closed: pam_kwallet5 needs the password to
unlock it, and there is not one. The lock screen has the same limitation.

Origin: https://github.com/sddm/sddm/pull/1220
